186
00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:47.486
Option C keeps the Grid
authoritative for agency zones

187
00:12:47.486 --> 00:12:51.498
and lets each cloud's native
DNS own its own private zones.

188
00:12:51.498 --> 00:12:55.534
The two connect by conditional
forwarding: a rule that says,

189
00:12:55.534 --> 00:12:58.747
for names ending in this
domain, ask that server.

190
00:12:58.747 --> 00:13:04.446
Inward: the Grid forwards cloud domains
to an inbound endpoint in AWS or Azure,

191
00:13:04.446 --> 00:13:08.008
or to an inbound server
policy address in Google,

192
00:13:08.008 --> 00:13:11.035
over Direct Connect, ExpressRoute or VPN.

193
00:13:11.035 --> 00:13:16.298
Outward: each cloud forwards agency
domains to the Grid, with an outbound

194
00:13:16.298 --> 00:13:21.648
endpoint and rules in AWS, a ruleset in
Azure, or a forwarding zone in Google.

195
00:13:21.648 --> 00:13:22.611
Facts to know.

196
00:13:22.611 --> 00:13:26.356
A Route 53 private hosted
zone answers only inside

197
00:13:26.356 --> 00:13:29.835
its linked VPCs or through
an inbound endpoint.

198
00:13:29.835 --> 00:13:34.273
Asked from anywhere else, the name
resolves on the public internet instead.

199
00:13:34.273 --> 00:13:39.846
In Azure, a network using Azure-provided
DNS checks linked private zones first,

200
00:13:39.846 --> 00:13:42.761
then forwarding rulesets, then Azure DNS.

201
00:13:42.761 --> 00:13:47.562
Google recommends exactly this
design: two authoritative systems,

202
00:13:47.562 --> 00:13:50.648
conditional forwarding, no zone transfers.

203
00:13:50.648 --> 00:13:54.162
In GovCloud, Route 53 public
and private DNS work,

204
00:13:54.162 --> 00:13:59.873
and Resolver endpoints and rules are
available, but Resolver delegation isn't.

205
00:13:59.873 --> 00:14:01.797
Addresses follow the same idea.

206
00:14:01.797 --> 00:14:06.247
The Grid owns the agency's address
blocks and hands each cloud a block.

207
00:14:06.247 --> 00:14:11.435
On commercial AWS, that handoff can
be automated through Universal DDI.

208
00:14:11.435 --> 00:14:15.969
In GovCloud, Azure and Google, it's
manual: reserve the block in the Grid,

209
00:14:15.969 --> 00:14:20.822
create the cloud pool with that range,
and let vDiscovery check the result.

210
00:14:20.822 --> 00:14:27.309
Sources: AWS Working with private hosted
zones, What is Route 53 VPC Resolver,

211
