216
00:14:54.492 --> 00:14:55.470
17, 2025.

217
00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:59.645
Whatever option you pick, four
rules prevent split-brain.

218
00:14:59.645 --> 00:15:03.083
One: every zone has
exactly one writable owner.

219
00:15:03.083 --> 00:15:09.307
That's the Grid, Route 53, Azure
Private DNS or Cloud DNS, never two.

220
00:15:09.307 --> 00:15:12.282
Everything else reaches
the owner by forwarding,

221
00:15:12.282 --> 00:15:14.895
or holds a copy that is
explicitly read-only.

222
00:15:14.895 --> 00:15:17.927
NIOS overwrites edits to
imported cloud zones,

223
00:15:17.927 --> 00:15:21.970
and portal-owned resolver rules
overwrite cloud-side changes.

224
00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:24.557
So decide ownership
before you turn sync on.

225
00:15:24.557 --> 00:15:27.282
Two: divide the namespace by owner.

226
00:15:27.282 --> 00:15:31.244
For example, corp dot agency
dot gov lives on the Grid,

227
00:15:31.244 --> 00:15:36.771
and aws dot corp dot agency dot gov
lives in a Route 53 private hosted zone.

228
00:15:36.771 --> 00:15:40.423
On-premises resolvers
forward the child to AWS,

229
00:15:40.423 --> 00:15:43.420
and AWS forwards the parent to the Grid.

230
00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:46.091
AWS also allows inbound delegation,

231
00:15:46.091 --> 00:15:50.008
but Resolver delegation
isn't available in GovCloud.

232
00:15:50.008 --> 00:15:53.223
And don't create a cloud
private zone with the same name

233
00:15:53.223 --> 00:15:56.582
as a Grid zone unless it's a
deliberate split-horizon view.

234
00:15:56.582 --> 00:16:01.745
Three: DNSSEC keys stay on the Grid
Master or its hardware security module.

235
00:16:01.745 --> 00:16:07.085
Route 53 can't sign private hosted zones,
and Universal DDI doesn't sync DNSSEC

236
00:16:07.085 --> 00:16:12.007
records, so don't sign in one system
and manage through sync from another.

237
00:16:12.007 --> 00:16:14.729
Validation belongs on agency resolvers;

238
00:16:14.729 --> 00:16:17.771
the Azure-provided
resolver doesn't validate.

239
00:16:17.771 --> 00:16:20.595
Four: IPAM authority stays on the Grid.

240
00:16:20.595 --> 00:16:22.517
Clouds get delegated blocks,

241
00:16:22.517 --> 00:16:27.082
and one network view for routable
space lets the Grid catch overlaps.

242
00:16:27.082 --> 00:16:34.097
Sources: Research synthesis, Avoiding
split-brain, DNSSEC keys and IPAM authority:

243
00:16:34.097 --> 00:16:37.905
Infoblox Route 53, Azure
DNS and GCP sync pages;

244
00:16:37.905 --> 00:16:44.218
Discovering Cloud Forwarder Endpoints;
AWS private hosted zones, Resolver,

245
00:16:44.218 --> 00:16:47.024
GovCloud Route 53, DNSSEC signing;

246
00:16:47.024 --> 00:16:51.634
Microsoft Learn DNS Private
Resolver and Azure DNSSEC;

247
00:16:51.634 --> 00:16:57.947
Google Cloud DNS best practices; Infoblox
DNSSEC on a Grid and Network Views;

248
00:16:57.947 --> 00:17:03.020
the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08, Chapter 02.

249
00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:05.107
The decision table.

250
00:17:05.107 --> 00:17:08.166
Every row is design
guidance, not an endorsement,

251
00:17:08.166 --> 00:17:10.307
resting on the facts we've covered.

252
00:17:10.307 --> 00:17:14.770
Mostly on premises, with a small
cloud footprint: Trinzic with native

253
00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:19.546
cloud resolvers and conditional
forwarding, the minimal form of Option C.

254
00:17:19.546 --> 00:17:23.175
It's the lowest cost, needs
no new SaaS authorization,

255
00:17:23.175 --> 00:17:25.707
and NIOS can't own cloud zones anyway.

256
00:17:25.707 --> 00:17:29.677
Hybrid with one cloud,
commercial or government:

257
00:17:29.677 --> 00:17:33.745
add an HA vNIOS pair in a
hub network, Option A plus C.

258
00:17:33.745 --> 00:17:38.426
It keeps resolution local if the WAN
fails and extends response policy,

259
00:17:38.426 --> 00:17:41.282
signed zones and Grid IPAM into the cloud.

260
00:17:41.282 --> 00:17:45.457
vNIOS is documented for both
GovCloud and Azure Government.

261
00:17:45.457 --> 00:17:48.217
Multi-cloud at FedRAMP Moderate and below:

262
00:17:48.217 --> 00:17:52.632
add Universal DDI on top, through
Government Cloud for federal work.

263
00:17:52.632 --> 00:17:56.616
It's the only Infoblox mechanism
that writes to cloud DNS,

264
00:17:56.616 --> 00:17:59.158
within the Government Cloud limits.

265
00:17:59.158 --> 00:18:02.492
GovCloud, Azure Government, FedRAMP High,

266
00:18:02.492 --> 00:18:07.396
or Defense Impact Levels 4 and 5:
stay with options A and C only.

267
00:18:07.396 --> 00:18:12.962
Infoblox's SaaS is Moderate-only, the
VPC IPAM integration excludes GovCloud,

268
00:18:12.962 --> 00:18:16.758
and native services carry
the higher authorizations.

269
00:18:16.758 --> 00:18:22.982
Google lists Cloud DNS for High,
IL4 and IL5 under Assured Workloads.

270
00:18:22.982 --> 00:18:28.295
For Azure Government, confirm DNS
Private Resolver is available first.

271
00:18:28.295 --> 00:18:33.554
In short, the design guidance is: Trinzic
plus native cloud resolvers as the

272
00:18:33.554 --> 00:18:38.983
baseline, with vNIOS and Universal DDI
added only where they earn their place.

273
00:18:38.983 --> 00:18:44.232
Sources: Research decision guidance
table and its cited sources:

274
00:18:44.232 --> 00:18:48.899
Infoblox vDiscovery, vNIOS for
AWS and Azure, Route 53 sync,

275
00:18:48.899 --> 00:18:53.370
Universal DDI integrations
and FedRAMP release notes,

276
00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:59.592
Universal Asset Insights discovery pages;
AWS Resolver, VPC IPAM + Infoblox,

277
00:18:59.592 --> 00:19:04.842
GovCloud Route 53; Google Assured
Workloads supported products;

278
00:19:04.842 --> 00:19:08.120
FedRAMP Marketplace products.json.

279
00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:09.957
Where does this fit?

280
00:19:09.957 --> 00:19:14.907
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 makes the vendor-neutral case.

281
00:19:14.907 --> 00:19:21.146
Chapter 02 explains split-brain, Chapter 07
covers cloud private endpoints and IPAM,

282
00:19:21.146 --> 00:19:25.651
Chapter 08 describes the policy
plane that overlays cloud DNS,

283
00:19:25.651 --> 00:19:28.683
and Chapter 12 covers platform selection.

284
00:19:28.683 --> 00:19:32.845
The research deep dive, Infoblox
DDI for Federal Agencies,

285
00:19:32.845 --> 00:19:38.220
covers the platform, its FedRAMP scope,
its risks, and how divisions share it.

286
00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:43.655
The decisions to take: which option for each
cloud and impact level; one named owner

287
00:19:43.655 --> 00:19:48.770
for every zone; where DNSSEC keys live;
and how address blocks reach each cloud.

288
00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:49.933
The next steps.

289
00:19:49.933 --> 00:19:53.610
Ask Infoblox for the Government
Cloud authorization boundary

290
00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:57.357
and a feature matrix for GovCloud
and Azure Government accounts.

291
00:19:57.357 --> 00:20:00.991
Confirm that DNS Private
Resolver and Virtual Network

292
00:20:00.991 --> 00:20:04.308
Manager IPAM are available
in Azure Government.

293
00:20:04.308 --> 00:20:07.945
Size NIOS-X capacity if
you want cloud forwarders.

294
00:20:07.945 --> 00:20:11.608
And test forwarding in both
directions from every origin.

295
00:20:11.608 --> 00:20:15.103
One note on standing: this
is research under review,

296
00:20:15.103 --> 00:20:17.933
not an endorsement by any federal agency.

297
00:20:17.933 --> 00:20:18.694
Thank you.

298
00:20:18.694 --> 00:20:25.582
Sources: Book 08, Chapters 02, 07,
08 and 12; research item Infoblox

